/**

@page page_exahype_visualisation Visualisation and output

\tableofcontents


ExaHyPE writes solution snapshots through `exahype2::plotter::PatchPlotter`
and the file writers described in @ref tarch_plotting. The runtime option
`--plotFormat` selects `vtu`, `vtkhdf`, `hdf5` or `netcdf`; the default is
VTKHDF when the build includes HDF5 and VTU otherwise.


# Solution output

Each snapshot contains the patches of the cells the solver owns and the named
unknowns and auxiliary variables in its project description. The cell fields
`RefinementLevel` and `Subdomain` record the level and the MPI rank of the
patch; limiting solvers also write `LimiterStatus`.

The plotter assigns each patch a fixed range of plot cells and points before
parallel filling. `postprocessing/render_curve.py` draws the cell centres
in snapshot order and colours them by `Subdomain`.


# Solver-specific rendering recommendations

## Finite Volumes

Each finite volume becomes one plot cell with cell-associated fields.
Patch boundaries appear where the refinement level or cell spacing changes.
Cell outlines are drawn by default; pass `--no-edges` to
`postprocessing/render_solution.py` to turn them off when you only want the field.


## Discontinuous Galerkin

Each DG node becomes one plot point at its quadrature position, and plot
cells connect neighbouring nodes within the patch. Gauss-Legendre nodes lie
inside a cell, so adjacent patches show visible gaps. A cell whose limiter
status selects the subcell solution plots the value of the containing subcell
at each DG node.


As with the Finite Volume output, cell outlines are drawn by default; pass
`--no-edges` to `postprocessing/render_solution.py` to turn them off.
Run `python postprocessing/render_solution.py --help` to see all available options,
including the field to plot (`--field`), the component of a vector field
(`--component`) and the colour map (`--colormap`).


# Comparing outputs

`postprocessing/compare_solution.py` and `postprocessing/check_symmetry.py`
ignore `Subdomain` by default because rank ownership changes with the
decomposition. `compare_solution.py --unordered` matches cells by geometry
when point numbering or boundary-point duplication differs between outputs.

`postprocessing/plot_error_norms.py` plots the error database, and
`postprocessing/plot_probe_data.py` plots tracer CSV records. See
@ref tarch_logging for terminal logging and profiling.



*/
